In **Wokwi**: an RGB LED (set its common pin to cathode), red on pin 9 via 330 Ω, green on 10 and blue on 11 via 220 Ω.

```arduino
const int R = 9, G = 10, B = 11;
int level = 0, step = 1;
unsigned long last = 0;

void setColor(int r, int g, int b) {
  analogWrite(R, r); analogWrite(G, g); analogWrite(B, b);
}

void setup() {}

void loop() {
  unsigned long now = millis();
  if (now - last >= 10) {          // every 10 ms, without blocking
    last = now;
    level += step;
    if (level == 0 || level == 255) step = -step;
    int v = (long)level * level / 255;   // rough perceptual correction
    setColor(v, 0, 255 - v);             // fade blue -> red and back
  }
}
```

Then switch the part to common anode in Wokwi and fix the code with `255 - value` in `setColor`. Try mixing: (255, 128, 0) should look orange, (255, 255, 255) white-ish.

Predict the three numbers this program prints. One function changes the caller's variable and one doesn't.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void add_one_copy(int x) { x += 1; }
void add_one_ref(int& x) { x += 1; }

int main() {
    int a = 10;
    add_one_copy(a);
    std::cout << a << '\n';
    add_one_ref(a);
    std::cout << a << '\n';
    int& alias = a;
    alias = 99;
    std::cout << a << '\n';
}
```

Python has one passing rule: names are bound to objects, and a function gets another name for the same object. Whether the caller "sees a change" depends on whether the object is mutable. C++ makes you choose explicitly, per parameter, and the choice is about **copies**.

### Pass by value

`void f(int x)` gets its own copy. Changes to `x` stay inside `f`. Copying an `int` is free; copying a `std::vector<double>` with a million readings means allocating and copying 8 MB every call.

### References

A reference is another name for an existing object. `int& r = a;` makes `r` an alias: writing `r = 99` writes `a`. Rules:

- A reference must be initialised when declared and can never be re-pointed at something else.
- There is no "null reference".
- `void f(int& x)` binds `x` to the caller's variable. That is how a function changes its argument. In the pretest: `10`, `11`, `99`.

### const references: the default for big inputs

`void print_stats(const std::vector<double>& v)` passes no copy *and* promises not to modify `v`. The compiler enforces the promise: `v.push_back(1.0)` inside would not compile. It also binds to temporaries, so `print_stats({1.0, 2.0})` works, which a plain `std::vector<double>&` would reject.

Rule of thumb:

| Parameter type | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `int`, `double`, `char`, `bool` | by value |
| something big you only read (`std::string`, `std::vector`) | `const T&` |
| something the function must change | `T&` |

### Range-for with references

```cpp-snippet
for (auto x : readings) x *= 2;          // doubles copies; readings unchanged
for (auto& x : readings) x *= 2;         // doubles the real elements
for (const auto& x : readings) sum += x; // reads without copying
```

The first form is a silent bug that looks right.

### Dangling references

A reference doesn't keep its object alive. Return a reference to a local and you hand back an alias to memory that no longer exists:

```cpp-snippet
const std::string& bad() {
    std::string s = "hello";
    return s;   // s dies at the closing brace; the caller holds a dangling reference
}
```

The compiler warns (Apple clang: `-Wreturn-stack-address`; GCC: `-Wreturn-local-addr`). Return by value instead; the compiler moves or elides the copy, so it's cheap.

### const and constexpr

- `const int max_speed = 255;` can't be changed after initialisation. The value may be known only at run time (`const int n = read_config();`).
- `constexpr int pwm_max = 255;` must be known at **compile time**, so it can size arrays and be used in other `constexpr` expressions. Prefer `constexpr` over `#define` for constants: it has a type and a scope.
- `const` on a reference parameter is the most common const you'll write.

### Worked example

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

constexpr int kMaxPwm = 255;

void clamp_pwm(std::vector<int>& duties) {
    for (auto& d : duties) {
        if (d < 0) d = 0;
        else if (d > kMaxPwm) d = kMaxPwm;
    }
}

double mean(const std::vector<int>& v) {
    if (v.empty()) return 0.0;
    long long sum = 0;
    for (const auto& x : v) sum += x;
    return static_cast<double>(sum) / v.size();
}

void min_max(const std::vector<int>& v, int& lo, int& hi) {
    lo = hi = v.at(0);
    for (int x : v) {
        if (x < lo) lo = x;
        if (x > hi) hi = x;
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> duties{-20, 100, 300, 255, 0};
    clamp_pwm(duties);                     // changes duties in place
    for (int d : duties) std::cout << d << ' ';
    std::cout << "\nmean " << mean(duties) << '\n';   // 122
    int lo = 0, hi = 0;
    min_max(duties, lo, hi);               // two results through out-parameters
    std::cout << lo << ".." << hi << '\n'; // 0..255
}
```

Read each signature and you know, without reading the body, which arguments can change. That is the real benefit: the type tells the reader the contract. `min_max` uses out-parameters; next weeks you'll see returning a `std::pair` or a small struct, which is usually clearer.

### Pointers, briefly

`int* p = &a;` stores an address; `*p = 5` writes through it. Pointers can be null and can be re-pointed, which is why references are preferred whenever neither is needed. Pointers get a full lesson in week 13.

