Text processing is everywhere in robotics code: serial commands from Python, config lines, sensor log rows. C++ gives you two types and the art is knowing which to use where.

### std::string: owns its characters

`std::string` is a growable array of `char` that manages its own memory, like Python's `str` but **mutable**.

```cpp-snippet
std::string s = "motor";
s += "_left";               // append: "motor_left"
s.size();                   // 10
s[0] = 'M';                 // mutable: "Motor_left"
s.substr(6, 4);             // "left": (start, count), NOT (start, end)
s.find('_');                // 5; returns std::string::npos if absent
s.find("left");             // 6
s == "Motor_left";          // true: == compares contents
std::to_string(42);         // "42"
std::stoi("-80");           // -80 (throws std::invalid_argument on "abc")
std::stod("3.5");           // 3.5
```

Two habits from Python to drop: `substr` takes a **length**, not an end index; and `find` returns `npos` (the largest `size_t`, 18446744073709551615 on 64-bit) rather than -1. That's the big number in the pretest. Always compare with `std::string::npos`.

The pretest prints: `11`, `5`, `120`, the huge npos value, then `1`.

### std::string_view: a window onto characters it doesn't own

`std::string_view` (header `<string_view>`) is just a pointer plus a length. Copying it is as cheap as copying two integers, and `substr` on a view makes another view without allocating. It can look at a `std::string`, a string literal or part of either.

Use it for **read-only parameters**:

```cpp-snippet
bool is_comment(std::string_view line) { return !line.empty() && line[0] == '#'; }
is_comment("# config");          // literal: no std::string is built
is_comment(some_std_string);     // implicit conversion: no copy
```

Compare with `const std::string&`: passing a literal there constructs a temporary `std::string` (an allocation for longer text). For read-only text parameters, `std::string_view` by value is the modern default.

### The one danger: dangling views

A view doesn't keep the characters alive. These are bugs:

```cpp-snippet
std::string_view v = std::string("temp");   // the temporary string dies at the semicolon
std::string_view name() { std::string s = "x"; return s; }  // s dies on return
```

Rules: never **store** a view or **return** one unless you're sure the owner outlives it. Parameters are safe because the caller's string lives for the whole call. If you need to keep text, convert to `std::string(view)`.

### Worked example: parse a motor command

Python will send lines like `L:120,R:-80`. Parse them into two ints.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

// Parses the integer after "<key>:" in a "K:v,K:v" line. Returns false if absent.
bool get_field(std::string_view line, char key, int& out) {
    std::size_t pos = 0;
    while (pos < line.size()) {
        std::size_t comma = line.find(',', pos);
        if (comma == std::string_view::npos) comma = line.size();
        std::string_view field = line.substr(pos, comma - pos);   // e.g. "R:-80"
        if (field.size() >= 3 && field[0] == key && field[1] == ':') {
            out = std::stoi(std::string(field.substr(2)));       // stoi needs a std::string
            return true;
        }
        pos = comma + 1;
    }
    return false;
}

int main() {
    std::string msg = "L:120,R:-80";
    int left = 0, right = 0;
    bool ok = get_field(msg, 'L', left) && get_field(msg, 'R', right);
    std::cout << ok << ' ' << left << ' ' << right << '\n';   // 1 120 -80
    int speed = 0;
    std::cout << get_field("S:5", 'X', speed) << '\n';         // 0
}
```

Points to notice: the loop walks the line field by field with `find` and `substr` on a view, so no allocation happens until the final conversion; `npos` is checked explicitly; the output parameter pattern from last lesson returns the number while the `bool` says whether it was found.

### C++20 conveniences

`s.starts_with("L:")` and `s.ends_with(".csv")` exist on both `std::string` and `std::string_view` from C++20. `std::string::contains` is C++23, so don't rely on it yet.

